air_refractive_index_590nm_288k.text:
{
  "entry_id": "air_refractive_index_590nm_288k",
  "schema_version": "0.1",
  "kind": "claim",
  "claim": "Dry air at 15 degrees Celsius and 760 mm of mercury has a refractive index of 1.000276466 at a wavelength of 590.0 nm, from interferometric measurements whose probable error at this temperature is 3.1 in the seventh decimal of the index.",
  "numbers": [
    {
      "name": "refractive_index",
      "value": 1.000276466,
      "unit": "1",
      "source": "literature:meggers_peters_1918",
      "grade": "E3",
      "precision": "significant_figures",
      "note": "Table 5 of the paper, 5900 A row, prints the refractivity (n-1)10^7 = 2764.66, which is n = 1.000276466. Rewriting a printed refractivity as an index is a change of form and not a derivation: no other quantity enters and nothing is fitted. The digits are kept as printed; the paper's probable error is 3.1 in (n-1)10^7 at this temperature, so the last two decimals shown are inside the noise, and rounding them away here would be this store deciding how much of another laboratory's number to believe. NEAR ENOUGH TO UNITY THAT THE DIFFERENCE MAY NOT MATTER: n-1 is 2.8e-4, so anything computed from this differs from the same thing computed with n = 1 by 0.03 per cent. Whether that is negligible is the caller's call, and the reason to hold the measured value anyway is the grade -- assuming unity would be an `assumed:` E5 and would cap whatever it feeds at E5, while this is E3."
    }
  ],
  "validity": {
    "wavelength": { "min": 590.0, "max": 590.0, "unit": "nm" },
    "temperature": { "min": 288.15, "max": 288.15, "unit": "K" },
    "pressure": { "min": 101325, "max": 101325, "unit": "Pa" }
  },
  "subject": [
    { "kind": "quantity", "id": "refractive_index" }
  ],
  "source": "literature:meggers_peters_1918",
  "grade": "E3",
  "grade_tag": "peer_reviewed",
  "source_ref": "src_meggers_peters_1918_air_index",
  "validity_conditions": "ONE WAVELENGTH, ONE TEMPERATURE, ONE PRESSURE, AND ALL THREE ARE PINNED TO POINTS. A query at any other comes back disjoint and the gap is `condition_mismatch`, not `absent`; the store may not interpolate. THE SOURCE PRINTS A 50 A GRID FROM 2000 A TO 10000 A, so any wavelength that lands on a grid point can be filed exactly and on request -- 6050 A among them, which is the emission path's nominal 605 nm centre. 5900 A was chosen because it is the grid point beside the sodium D line, where water's index is also stated, so the two immersion media are held in the same spectral neighbourhood. THE SIZE OF EACH MISMATCH, so a caller can judge rather than guess: across 5400 to 6150 A the printed refractivity moves from 2772.75 to 2761.40, i.e. n changes by 1.1e-6 over that whole span; the paper's three temperature sets give (n-1)10^7 of 2875.66, 2726.43 and 2589.72 at 0, 15 and 30 degC at infinite wavelength, so the temperature dependence is about -1e-6 per kelvin; and refractivity is very nearly proportional to density, so a 1 kPa departure from 101.325 kPa moves n by about 2.7e-6. All three are far below the precision an axial range is quoted to, and saying so is the caller's judgement to record, not this entry's to apply. DRY AIR. The measurement is on dry air; the air over this bench carries water vapour, which lowers the index by of order 1e-6 per 10 per cent relative humidity in the visible. Humidity is not a quantity in `validity` because the source does not tabulate it, and asserting a bound the source does not state would be worse than leaving it to prose. TEMPERATURE IS IN KELVIN BECAUSE THE REGISTRY HAS NO OTHER OPTION: contracts/units.json cannot register an offset unit and says temperature is carried in kelvin everywhere, so the source's 15 degC is carried as 288.15 K and its 760 mm of mercury as the 101325 Pa that defines the standard atmosphere. Both conversions are exact. WHY IT WAS SOURCED. Three of the six objectives in the device table are dry (MRD70040, MRD70170, MRD70270, `immersion: air`), and A6's axial_range needs the index of the medium they work in. `immersion: air` is on this entry so those objectives and this index come back from one kb_query on the class handle (task 024).",
  "date": "2026-09-20",
  "supersedes": null,
  "conflict_with": [],
  "curated_by": "seat:librarian",
  "identifiers": {
    "immersion": "air",
    "material": "air",
    "table_row": "5900"
  }
}

air_refractive_index_595nm_288k.text:
{
  "entry_id": "air_refractive_index_595nm_288k",
  "schema_version": "0.1",
  "kind": "claim",
  "claim": "Dry air at 15 degrees Celsius and 760 mm of mercury has a refractive index of 1.000276398 at a wavelength of 595.0 nm, from interferometric measurements whose probable error at this temperature is 3.1 in the seventh decimal of the index.",
  "numbers": [
    {
      "name": "refractive_index",
      "value": 1.000276398,
      "unit": "1",
      "source": "literature:meggers_peters_1918",
      "grade": "E3",
      "precision": "significant_figures",
      "note": "Table 5 of the paper, 5950 A row, prints the refractivity (n-1)10^7 = 2763.98, which is n = 1.000276398. An exact grid point on the table's 50 A step, so nothing is interpolated. Cross-checked inside its own row: the table prints the vacuum correction (n*lambda - lambda) = 1.6446 A, and 5950 A x 2763.98e-7 = 1.64457 A. The probable error is 3.1 in (n-1)10^7, so the last two decimals shown sit inside the noise; they are kept as printed."
    }
  ],
  "validity": {
    "wavelength": { "min": 595.0, "max": 595.0, "unit": "nm" },
    "temperature": { "min": 288.15, "max": 288.15, "unit": "K" },
    "pressure": { "min": 101325, "max": 101325, "unit": "Pa" }
  },
  "subject": [
    { "kind": "quantity", "id": "refractive_index" }
  ],
  "source": "literature:meggers_peters_1918",
  "grade": "E3",
  "grade_tag": "peer_reviewed",
  "source_ref": "src_meggers_peters_1918_air_index",
  "validity_conditions": "WHY 595 AND NOT ONLY 605. The person settled on 2026-09-20 that 605 is the bench's name for the FF01-595/31-32 filter rather than a separate band: the store holds four single-band emission filters -- 432/36, 515/30, 595/31, 680/42 -- and no 605 filter. So the wavelength with a source of its own is 595, from filter_ff01_595_31_32_passband at E3 vendor_spec, and 605 is a nickname that gets no entry. air_refractive_index_605nm_288k stays, equally measured and equally a grid point; 595 against 605 is 1.7 per cent on a quantity reported to one significant figure, a tie under P15, so the two differ in which query matches and not in the physics. Both are filed so the store answers either without interpolating. THE TEMPERATURE IS STILL NOT THE ROOM'S, and that is the axis this source cannot close: it measured dry air at 0, 15 and 30 degC and printed a wavelength table at 15 degC alone, so a query at bench temperature comes back disjoint on temperature and the gap is `condition_mismatch`, not `absent`. THE SIZE OF EVERY MISMATCH IS STATED SO THE CALLER CAN JUDGE IT. The paper's three Cauchy equations give (n-1)10^7 of 2875.66, 2726.43 and 2589.72 at 0, 15 and 30 degC, so about -1e-6 per kelvin: five kelvin is about 5e-6 in n against an n-1 of 2.8e-4. Refractivity is nearly proportional to density, so 1 kPa off 101.325 kPa moves n by about 2.7e-6. And the measurement is on DRY air while the bench's air carries water vapour, which lowers n by of order 1e-6 per 10 per cent relative humidity in the visible; humidity is absent from `validity` because the source tabulates none, and asserting an unstated bound would be worse than prose. Deciding whether any of this matters is the caller's, and the entry's job is to make the decision possible. Temperature and pressure are in registry units: 15 degC as 288.15 K, 760 mm of mercury as the 101325 Pa that defines the standard atmosphere, both exact. WHY IT WAS SOURCED: three of the six objectives are dry -- MRD70040, MRD70170, MRD70270 -- and A6's axial_range needs the index of the medium they work in (task 026).",
  "date": "2026-09-20",
  "supersedes": null,
  "conflict_with": [],
  "curated_by": "seat:librarian",
  "identifiers": {
    "immersion": "air",
    "material": "air",
    "table_row": "5950"
  }
}

