camera_bodies_are_told_apart_by_serial.text:
{
  "entry_id": "camera_bodies_are_told_apart_by_serial",
  "schema_version": "0.1",
  "kind": "claim",
  "claim": "The two camera bodies are distinguished by serial number rather than by label: A23H723003 is the blue arm and A24M723015 is the red arm.",
  "identifiers": {
    "serial": "A23H723003",
    "model": "Kinetix 22"
  },
  "subject": [
    {
      "kind": "device",
      "id": "camera_red"
    },
    {
      "kind": "device",
      "id": "camera_blue"
    }
  ],
  "source": "prior_run:agentic-microscope@safety-2026-09-03",
  "grade": "E3",
  "source_ref": "src_prior_safety_memo",
  "valid_until": {
    "date": "2036-09-17",
    "event": "either body is replaced, OR the two bodies are moved between arms. THE SECOND HALF IS WHY THIS IS NOT cameras_both_kinetix22's EVENT: this claim is a mapping from two serial numbers to two arms, so exchanging the bodies falsifies it while leaving `both are Kinetix 22` true and leaving every pixel size untouched. A reader treating the two camera entries as one fact with one expiry gets this one wrong. WHAT A STALE MAPPING COSTS: the serials are the only way to tell the bodies apart -- this claim says so itself, that they are distinguished by serial rather than by label -- so it misroutes every per-arm fact with nothing to contradict it. THE DATE IS AN OUTER BOUND on the same reasoning as cameras_both_kinetix22, ten years from 2026-09-17, and nobody stated it for this entry either."
  },
  "validity_conditions": "Ruled TRANSFER under 10.2.1 and passed 10.3 rule 1, so it caps at E3 -- a run taken in another project is not a run taken here. 10.2's hardware-control-path row is open and says in its own rule that it does not wait for envelope/, because wrapping a control path needs no safety limit. Slot: O1 preflight, which has to know which body it opened. The labels are the thing not to trust: this store already records that the two bodies are the same model, so nothing about a frame distinguishes them, and it already records one instance of this instrument's paper record diverging from its hardware. A serial is addressable, so either number now finds this entry.",
  "date": "2026-09-19",
  "supersedes": null,
  "conflict_with": [],
  "curated_by": "kyuhwan"
}

camera_qe_at_600nm_from_the_kinetix_grap.text:
{
  "schema_version": "0.1",
  "kind": "claim",
  "date": "2026-09-20",
  "supersedes": null,
  "conflict_with": [],
  "curated_by": "seat:librarian",
  "source": "prior_run:agentic-microscope@34a432c",
  "grade": "E3",
  "source_ref": "src_prior_detector_curve_and_branch_20260920",
  "entry_id": "camera_qe_at_600nm_from_the_kinetix_graph",
  "claim": "At 600 nm the quantum efficiency carried for the Photometrics Kinetix22 is 0.96, and it is not the Kinetix22's own figure: it is one point of the Kinetix's datasheet graph, read off by eye, borrowed as a one-time exception authorised by the person on 2026-08-19.",
  "numbers": [
    {
      "name": "quantum_efficiency",
      "value": 0.96,
      "unit": "1",
      "source": "prior_run:agentic-microscope@34a432c",
      "grade": "E3",
      "precision": "order_of_magnitude",
      "note": "the 600 nm point of the borrowed curve, which is tabulated on a coarse grid from 200 to 1100 nm. `order_of_magnitude` is declared deliberately: the curve was read off a printed graph by eye and the source marks it `qe_verified: false`, so two decimals are what was written down and not what is known. 600 nm is the grid point beside the band this path collects; any other tabulated point can be filed on request, and none of them is measured here."
    }
  ],
  "validity": {
    "wavelength": {
      "min": 600.0,
      "max": 600.0,
      "unit": "nm"
    }
  },
  "validity_conditions": "ONE POINT, NOT A CURVE, AND THE NAME SAYS SO NOW. This entry was filed as `camera_qe_curve_is_the_kinetix_graph` earlier today and renamed the same day, because quantities.json's `quantum_efficiency` note ruled that THE CURVE IS NOT AN ENTRY AND THERE IS NO FIELD FOR ONE, and listed the legal shapes: a scalar, points a PERSON read as one entry each with the wavelength pinned, or a vendor numeric table beside the other tables. This is the second shape and always was -- one value, `validity.wavelength` pinned to 600 nm, read by a person and not by a model, which is what keeps it out of 5.3's E6. What was wrong was the identifier: a reader greping the store would have found a name promising a curve. That is task 015's shape, a claim living in an entry name where nothing checks it, and it is why the id moved rather than the content. Nothing cited it -- checked before renaming -- and the claim is unchanged, so this is a rename and not a supersede; rule 8 keeps a replaced CLAIM, and no claim was replaced. THE CURVE IS NOT RESCALED AND THAT REFUSAL IS THE POINT OF THIS ENTRY. It peaks at 0.97 while the Kinetix22's own datasheet states 0.95, and the source declined to normalise one to the other because, in its words, the 2 per cent gap sits well inside the error of reading a graph by eye, so normalising the peak `would imply a precision neither source has` and would break traceability back to the Kinetix graph. THAT IS CARRIED HERE SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT DO THE ARITHMETIC THE SOURCE DECLINED TO DO. Scaling this curve by 0.95/0.97 produces a number with five figures of apparent agreement and no more evidence than it started with. IT IS THE WRONG BODY'S GRAPH, and that is a substitution the caller is making, not a fact this entry establishes. The two cameras share a sensor family and differ in array size; whether their QE curves coincide is not something either datasheet says. The same shape as the polystyrene plates standing in for beads. IT IS NOT camera_qe_peak. That entry is the Kinetix22's own documented 0.95 at E3 vendor_spec and survives if this curve is withdrawn; this one is a borrowed graph at E3 prior_run. They are separate files because they have different sources, different subjects and different failure modes, and because the source itself keeps them apart. WHAT WOULD RAISE IT: a real digitisation of the Kinetix22's own QE curve, which the source names as the action that would let it set `qe_verified: true` -- asking Photometrics for the curve as CSV. That is an action outside this repository. Slot: A1. Rule passed: 10.3 rule 1.",
  "subject": [
    {
      "kind": "device",
      "id": "camera_red"
    }
  ],
  "identifiers": {
    "model": "Kinetix 22"
  }
}

cameras_both_kinetix22.text:
{
  "entry_id": "cameras_both_kinetix22",
  "schema_version": "0.1",
  "kind": "claim",
  "claim": "Both cameras are Kinetix 22. They are the same model, so pixel size, quantum efficiency, read noise and full-well capacity are the same on both arms, and the two arms of the splitter are photometrically comparable without a cross-calibration term. The names red and blue, and the roles primary and second detector, are wiring and naming rather than a hardware difference: neither camera is the better one.",
  "valid_until": {
    "date": "2036-09-17",
    "event": "either camera body is replaced by one of a different model. THE MECHANISM: this entry is what binds camera_sensor_geometry's 6.5 um pitch to THIS BENCH -- that entry is a claim about what a Kinetix 22 IS and holds wherever one sits, while this one says the two things here are Kinetix 22s, and only the second can stop being true. Twelve E2 pixel sizes name a camera change as their terminating event and reach the pitch through this sentence, so an unmarked expiry here was the hole those twelve were just given an event to close. THE FALSIFIER WAS ALREADY WRITTEN IN THIS ENTRY'S OWN PROSE -- `Falsified by either camera being replaced` -- and never reached a field anything could read; that is the same shape as the projection defects of 030 and 031, one level out. IT ALSO CARRIES A SECOND CLAIM THE SAME EVENT ENDS: that the two arms are photometrically comparable with no cross-calibration term. That follows from same-model and dies with it. NOT ENDED BY SWAPPING THE TWO BODIES BETWEEN ARMS, since they are the same model and both halves stay true -- which is exactly what camera_bodies_are_told_apart_by_serial's event does cover. Two camera entries, two different events; they are not one fact. THE DATE IS AN OUTER BOUND AND NOT A MEASUREMENT: ten years from the operator's reading of 2026-09-17, the interval this store already uses twice for this job (particle_suspension_a_identity, particles_show_on_the_green_605_path) where the event is the real condition and the date only has to outlast it. Nobody stated it for this entry."
  },
  "validity_conditions": "This instrument as configured on 2026-09-17, operator read at the instrument. This closes an older discrepancy in which a purchase quote listed one Kinetix 22 and one Prime95B against a current state recorded as two Kinetix, unconfirmed: a swap did happen against the quote. Sensor properties are shared because the model is shared; no sensor number is entered here, and the calibrated pixel size remains a per-configuration gap. Falsified by either camera being replaced.",
  "identifiers": {
    "model": "Kinetix 22"
  },
  "source": "operator_read:kyuhwan_20260917",
  "grade_tag": "operator_read",
  "subject": [
    { "kind": "device", "id": "camera_red" },
    { "kind": "device", "id": "camera_blue" }
  ],
  "grade": "E3",
  "source_ref": "src_operator_read_20260917",
  "date": "2026-09-17",
  "supersedes": null,
  "conflict_with": [],
  "curated_by": "kyuhwan"
}

